    "result_text": "All tests pass (the suite consists of the single `AcceptanceTest`, which is now green). The full build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the \"possible duplicate\" (soft-match) rule for owner creation. A new owner that isn't a hard duplicate but shares an existing owner's `lastName` and `postcode` with a *different* telephone is still created, and comes back with `possibleDuplicate: true` and `possibleDuplicateOf` set to the matching owner's id.\n\nChanges, each kept in its proper layer:\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added two read-only fields to the `Owner` schema: `possibleDuplicate` (boolean) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (int32). These regenerate into `OwnerDto`, and MapStruct maps them automatically since the names match the model.\n- **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added the persistent `possibleDuplicate` / `possibleDuplicateOf` columns with getters/setters, alongside the existing derived-at-creation fields (`bulkSignupWarning`, `namesakeCount`, etc.). They must be persisted because the test reads them back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n- **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 added the `possible_duplicate` / `possible_duplicate_of` columns (tests run under `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the DDL is required).\n- **`service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`** \u2014 added a single-responsibility `flagPossibleDuplicate(owner)` helper, invoked during `createOwner` after the hard-duplicate rejection. It reuses the existing `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` repository query, filters candidates by matching postcode and differing telephone, and picks the lowest-id match (deterministic when several match); an owner with no postcode can never soft-match.\n\nThe logic sits in the service (which already owns the other creation-time enrichments and repository lookups), reuses the existing query, and doesn't bloat any class with unrelated responsibility.",
